# End-to-end QEMU coverage of the boot-time hugepage reservation (spec-vpp-host-tuning AC-3).
#
# Drives scripts/evidence/effective-vpp-hugepages-qemu.py, which builds a HOST ze,
# inits an appliance whose config carries image.hugepages + image.memory-bytes,
# builds the gokrazy image (exercising the derived-instance-config kernel-argument
# path), boots it in QEMU, logs in over SSH, and asserts through the Ze CLI that
# `show host kernel | json` carries the reserved-pages arguments and that
# `show host memory | json` reports hugepages-total >= 1. The appliance's SSH
# server IS the Ze CLI, so shell commands like `cat /proc/cmdline` are rejected.
#
# The driver self-skips with a clear reason when QEMU, sshpass, e2fsprogs, or the
# go toolchain are absent. It exits 0 in both PASS and SKIP cases; a genuine
# mismatch exits non-zero, as does an appliance that never answers under a
# hardware accelerator (kvm/hvf). On Linux that needs the invoking user in the
# `kvm` group -- `make ze-setup` checks this as kvm-access. The exhaustive run is
# `make ze-vpp-hugepages-qemu-test`.

# The auto-timeout is derived from this test's rolling baseline (5x the average).
# On a host without KVM the driver SKIPs in a couple of seconds, so the baseline
# settles around 3s and the derived budget lands near 15s -- far too short for the
# real work this test does when the prerequisites ARE present: a host ze build, an
# appliance init, a full gokrazy image build, and a QEMU boot. That mismatch killed
# the run at 15s on the first KVM-capable host to meet it. An explicit timeout
# always wins over the derived one, so pin it to the real cost.
option=timeout:value=900s
cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=python3 scripts/evidence/effective-vpp-hugepages-qemu.py
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:regex=VPP-HUGEPAGES-QEMU: (PASS|SKIP)
